anytime with:\\n    - bash /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh\\n\\n- Weekly cron:\\n  - Path: /etc/cron.d/rotate-authorized-keys\\n  - Entry:\\n    - SHELL=/bin/sh\\n    - PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin\\n    - MAILTO=root\\n    - 17 3 * * 0 root bash /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh\\n  - Runs every Sunday at 03:17.\\n  - Note: Cron must be installed and running for this to take effect (e.g., systemctl enable --now cron on Debian/Ubuntu, or crond on RHEL/CentOS/AlmaLinux).\\n\\nNotes\\n\\n- The script targets only /home/*/.ssh/authorized_keys as requested. It does not touch root\u2019s or system users\u2019 keys.\\n- Backups are created before removal to avoid locking out valid access while you re-issue keys.\\n- There is an older, unused script file at /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys.sh (created initially). The cron job points to the fixed script, so it\u2019s safe, but you can remove that older file if you wish.\\n\\nHow to verify\\n\\n- If users exist under /home with authorized_keys files:\\n  - Run: bash /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh\\n  - Check backups: ls -R /root/ssh-keys-backup\\n  - Verify the files are empty: find /home -maxdepth 3 -type f -name authorized_keys -exec ls -l {} \\\\; -exec cat {} \\\\;\\n\\nNext steps for you\\n\\n- Ensure the cron service is active on this host.\\n- Re-issue and distribute new SSH keys to users as needed by writing new entries into their authorized_keys files after the rotation.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"That's a thorough summary you've provided. Is there something specific you need help with related to this task? Maybe a review or additional instructions?\", image_urls=None, wait_for_response=False, action='message', security_risk=None), RecallAction(recall_type='knowledge', query=\"That's a thorough summary you've provided. Is there something specific you need help with related to this task? Maybe a review or additional instructions?\", thought='', action='recall'), MessageAction(content='Thanks! A few quick things you could confirm or decide so I can finalize this:\\n\\nScope and policy\\n- Include system users or root in rotation, or keep it strictly /home/* as now?\\n- Any users to exclude (service accounts, CI users)?\\n- Backup retention: how long should /root/ssh-keys-backup be kept? Want me to add cleanup?\\n- After rotation, how will new keys be distributed? Want me to add a placeholder notice or automation?\\n\\nOperational details\\n- Is cron running on this host, or would you prefer a systemd timer instead?\\n- Do you want email or log notifications after each run? If so, preferred destination?\\n- Should I add a dry-run mode and more verbose logging?\\n\\nSecurity hardening (optional)\\n- Lock down SSH config further (disable PasswordAuthentication, restrict PermitRootLogin, etc.)?\\n- Restrict file permissions more aggressively on ~/.ssh dirs?\\n\\nCleanup\\n- Should I remove the initial unused script (/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys.sh) and keep only the fixed one?\\n\\nIf you share your preferences, I can:\\n- Switch to a systemd timer,\\n- Add reporting and retention cleanup,\\n- Implement an allow/deny list for users,\\n- Add a dry-run and better logs,\\n- Harden SSH settings,\\n- Test with a mock user to demonstrate backups and truncation.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"Those are all great follow-up points! Let's address them one by one:\\n\\n### Scope and Policy\\n1. **Include system users or root in rotation, or keep it strictly /home/* as now?**\\n   - I would recommend keeping it strictly to `/home/*` users for now, as including root or system users may affect critical operations. However, if you have a specific reason for rotating those keys, consider handling them with a separate, more cautious script.\\n\\n2. **Any users to exclude (service accounts, CI users)?**\\n   - Generally, it\u2019s good practice to exclude service accounts and CI users from such rotations unless absolutely necessary. Check if there are any critical users that must maintain constant access.\\n\\n3. **Backup retention: how long should /root/ssh-keys-backup be kept? Want me to add cleanup?**\\n   - A backup retention period of 30 days is typically reasonable unless there's a compliance requirement for longer retention. You should definitely add a cleanup mechanism to manage storage usage effectively.\\n\\n4. **After rotation, how will new keys be distributed? Want me to add a placeholder notice or automation?**\\n   - Automating new key distribution can be complex and might need a secure mechanism for key transfer. For now, a placeholder notice instructing users how to re-add their keys might be sufficient. You can introduce automation later if required.\\n\\n### Operational Details\\n1. **Is cron running on this host, or would you prefer a systemd timer instead?**\\n   - Cron is usually reliable, but systemd timers offer more flexibility and are easier to manage in some setups. Choose based on your system's existing practices.\\n\\n2. **Do you want email or log notifications after each run? If so, preferred destination?**\\n   - Email notifications are useful for making sure the script ran successfully. Sending them to `root` or an admin email would be ideal. Alternatively, logging to a dedicated log file could be another option.\\n\\n3. **Should I add a dry-run mode and more verbose logging?**\\n   - Dry-run mode is a great safety measure to verify the script\u2019s behavior without making changes. More verbose logging can also help in debugging and auditing.\\n\\n### Security Hardening (Optional)\\n1. **Lock down SSH config further (disable PasswordAuthentication, restrict PermitRootLogin, etc.)?**\\n   - Yes, hardening SSH configurations is always a good security practice. Disabling `PasswordAuthentication` and restricting `PermitRootLogin` to only essential users is recommended.\\n\\n2. **Restrict file permissions more aggressively on ~/.ssh dirs?**\\n   - Yes, ensuring that `.ssh` directories and files within have stringent permissions can help prevent unauthorized access.\\n\\n### Cleanup\\n1. **Should I remove the initial unused script (/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys.sh) and keep only the fixed one?**\\n   - Yes, keeping only the functional script helps in avoiding confusion and potential errors.\\n\\nImplementing these preferences could make your setup more robust and secure. Let me know how you would like to proceed!\", image_urls=None, wait_for_response=False, action='message', security_risk=None), RecallAction(recall_type='knowledge', query=\"Those are all great follow-up points! Let's address them one by one:\\n\\n### Scope and Policy\\n1. **Include system users or root in rotation, or keep it strictly /home/* as now?**\\n   - I would recommend keeping it strictly to `/home/*` users for now, as including root or system users may affect critical operations. However, if you have a specific reason for rotating those keys, consider handling them with a separate, more cautious script.\\n\\n2. **Any users to exclude (service accounts, CI users)?**\\n   - Generally, it\u2019s good practice to exclude service accounts and CI users from such rotations unless absolutely necessary. Check if there are any critical users that must maintain constant access.\\n\\n3. **Backup retention: how long should /root/ssh-keys-backup be kept? Want me to add cleanup?**\\n   - A backup retention period of 30 days is typically reasonable unless there's a compliance requirement for longer retention. You should definitely add a cleanup mechanism to manage storage usage effectively.\\n\\n4. **After rotation, how will new keys be distributed? Want me to add a placeholder notice or automation?**\\n   - Automating new key distribution can be complex and might need a secure mechanism for key transfer. For now, a placeholder notice instructing users how to re-add their keys might be sufficient. You can introduce automation later if required.\\n\\n### Operational Details\\n1. **Is cron running on this host, or would you prefer a systemd timer instead?**\\n   - Cron is usually reliable, but systemd timers offer more flexibility and are easier to manage in some setups. Choose based on your system's existing practices.\\n\\n2. **Do you want email or log notifications after each run? If so, preferred destination?**\\n   - Email notifications are useful for making sure the script ran successfully. Sending them to `root` or an admin email would be ideal. Alternatively, logging to a dedicated log file could be another option.\\n\\n3. **Should I add a dry-run mode and more verbose logging?**\\n   - Dry-run mode is a great safety measure to verify the script\u2019s behavior without making changes. More verbose logging can also help in debugging and auditing.\\n\\n### Security Hardening (Optional)\\n1. **Lock down SSH config further (disable PasswordAuthentication, restrict PermitRootLogin, etc.)?**\\n   - Yes, hardening SSH configurations is always a good security practice. Disabling `PasswordAuthentication` and restricting `PermitRootLogin` to only essential users is recommended.\\n\\n2. **Restrict file permissions more aggressively on ~/.ssh dirs?**\\n   - Yes, ensuring that `.ssh` directories and files within have stringent permissions can help prevent unauthorized access.\\n\\n### Cleanup\\n1. **Should I remove the initial unused script (/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys.sh) and keep only the fixed one?**\\n   - Yes, keeping only the functional script helps in avoiding confusion and potential errors.\\n\\nImplementing these preferences could make your setup more robust and secure. Let me know how you would like to proceed!\", thought='', action='recall'), FileReadAction(path='/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh', start=0, end=-1, thought='', action='read', security_risk=None, impl_source='oh_aci', view_range=[1, -1]), FileReadObservation(content='Here\\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh:\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     3\\tumask 077\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\ttimestamp=$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)\\n     6\\tbackup_root=\"/root/ssh-keys-backup/$timestamp\"\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\tfind /home -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 3 -type f -name authorized_keys -path \"/home/*/.ssh/*\" -print0 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r -d \\'\\' f; do\\n     9\\t  userdir=\"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$f\")\")\"\\n    10\\t  user=\"$(basename \"$userdir\")\"\\n    11\\t  if [ -s \"$f\" ]; then\\n    12\\t    mkdir -p \"$backup_root/$user\"\\n    13\\t    cp -p -- \"$f\" \"$backup_root/$user/authorized_keys\"\\n    14\\t  fi\\n    15\\t  : > \"$f\"\\n    16\\t  chown \"$user\":\"$user\" \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\\n    17\\t  chmod 600 \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\\n    18\\t  echo \"Rotated keys for user: $user ($f)\"\\n    19\\tdone\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\texit 0\\n    22\\t\\n', path='/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh', observation='read', impl_source='oh_aci'), **FileEditAction**\nPath: [/usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh]\nThought: \nCommand: str_replace\nOld String: ```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\numask 077\n\ntimestamp=$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)\nbackup_root=\"/root/ssh-keys-backup/$timestamp\"\n\nfind /home -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 3 -type f -name authorized_keys -path \"/home/*/.ssh/*\" -print0 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do\n  userdir=\"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$f\")\")\"\n  user=\"$(basename \"$userdir\")\"\n  if [ -s \"$f\" ]; then\n    mkdir -p \"$backup_root/$user\"\n    cp -p -- \"$f\" \"$backup_root/$user/authorized_keys\"\n  fi\n  : > \"$f\"\n  chown \"$user\":\"$user\" \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  chmod 600 \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  echo \"Rotated keys for user: $user ($f)\"\ndone\n\nexit 0\n\n```\nNew String: ```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\numask 077\n\n# SSH authorized_keys rotation utility\n# - Backs up authorized_keys under /root/ssh-keys-backup/TIMESTAMP/USER\n# - Truncates files and writes a short notice comment\n# - Fixes ownership and permissions\n# - Supports excludes, dry-run, and 30-day backup retention\n\nDRY_RUN=0\nVERBOSE=0\nRETENTION_DAYS=${RETENTION_DAYS:-30}\nEXCLUDE_FILE=${EXCLUDE_FILE:-/etc/ssh-rotate/exclude-users}\n\nusage() {\n  cat <<USAGE\nUsage: $(basename \"$0\") [options]\n  -n, --dry-run            Show actions without changing anything\n  -v, --verbose            More logging\n      --retention-days N   Override backup retention (default: ${RETENTION_DAYS})\n  -h, --help               Show this help\n\nEnvironment overrides:\n  RETENTION_DAYS, EXCLUDE_FILE, EXCLUDE_USERS\nUSAGE\n}\n\n# Parse arguments\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case \"${1}\" in\n    -n|--dry-run) DRY_RUN=1 ;;\n    -v|--verbose) VERBOSE=1 ;;\n    --retention-days) shift; RETENTION_DAYS=\"${1:-$RETENTION_DAYS}\" ;;\n    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;\n    *) echo \"Unknown option: $1\" >&2; usage; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\n  shift || true\ndone\n\nlog() { echo \"$@\"; }\n\ntimestamp=$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)\nbackup_root=\"/root/ssh-keys-backup/$timestamp\"\n\n# Build exclude list\nEXCLUDES=\" ${EXCLUDE_USERS:-} \"\nif [ -f \"$EXCLUDE_FILE\" ]; then\n  while IFS= read -r line; do\n    case \"$line\" in ''|'#'*) continue ;; esac\n    EXCLUDES=\"${EXCLUDES} ${line} \"\n  done < \"$EXCLUDE_FILE\"\nfi\n\nis_excluded() {\n  case \" $EXCLUDES \" in\n    *\" $1 \"*) return 0 ;;\n    *) return 1 ;;\n  esac\n}\n\nrotated_any=0\n\n# Iterate over authorized_keys files\nfind /home -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 3 -type f -name authorized_keys -path \"/home/*/.ssh/*\" -print0 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do\n  userdir=\"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$f\")\")\"\n  user=\"$(basename \"$userdir\")\"\n  sshdir=\"$(dirname \"$f\")\"\n\n  if is_excluded \"$user\"; then\n    [ \"$VERBOSE\" = \"1\" ] && log \"Skipping excluded user: $user\"\n    continue\n  fi\n\n  log \"Rotating keys for user: $user ($f)\"\n  rotated_any=1\n\n  if [ \"$DRY_RUN\" = \"1\" ]; then\n    [ -s \"$f\" ] && log \"  Would backup to $backup_root/$user/authorized_keys\"\n    log \"  Would truncate and write notice to $f\"\n    log \"  Would fix perms on $sshdir (700) and $f (600)\"\n    continue\n  fi\n\n  if [ -s \"$f\" ]; then\n    mkdir -p \"$backup_root/$user\"\n    cp -p -- \"$f\" \"$backup_root/$user/authorized_keys\"\n  fi\n\n  {\n    printf '# SSH keys were rotated on %s\\n' \"$(date -u +\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\"\n    printf '# Please re-add your public key or contact an administrator.\\n'\n  } > \"$f\"\n\n  chown \"$user\":\"$user\" \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  chmod 600 \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  chown \"$user\":\"$user\" \"$sshdir\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  chmod 700 \"$sshdir\" 2>/dev/null || true\n\ndone\n\n# Cleanup old backups (older than RETENTION_DAYS)\nif [ \"$DRY_RUN\" = \"1\" ]; then\n  log \"Would remove backups older than ${RETENTION_DAYS} days from /root/ssh-keys-backup\"\nelse\n  find /root/ssh-keys-backup -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -type d -mtime +\"${RETENTION_DAYS}\" -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null || true\nfi\n\nif [ \"$rotated_any\" = \"0\" ]; then\n  log \"No authorized_keys files found under /home. Nothing to do.\"\nfi\n\nexit 0\n\n```\n, FileEditObservation(content='The file /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh has been edited. Here\\'s the result of running `cat -n` on a snippet of /usr/local/sbin/rotate_authorized_keys_fixed.sh:\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     3\\tumask 077\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t# SSH authorized_keys rotation utility\\n     6\\t# - Backs up authorized_keys under /root/ssh-keys-backup/TIMESTAMP/USER\\n     7\\t# - Truncates files and writes a short notice comment\\n     8\\t# - Fixes ownership and permissions\\n     9\\t# - Supports excludes, dry-run, and 30-day backup 